Article 17
The Minimum Service Standards as referred to in Article 16 have performance indicators comprising:
a. coverage of Women and Child Victims of Violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers in integrated service units;
b. coverage of Women and Child Victims of Violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Community Health Centers and at Hospitals; provided by
c. coverage of Social Rehabilitation services provided by trained Social Rehabilitation officers for Women and Child Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Child Victims of Violence in integrated service units;
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level up to court decisions on cases of Violence against Women and Children;
f. coverage of Women and Child Victims of Violence who receive legal aid services;
g. coverage of Repatriation services for Women and Child Victims of Violence; and
h. coverage of social reintegration services for Women and Child Victims of Violence.

Article 15
(1) In providing Protection to Women and Child Victims of Violence, the Integrated Service Center is obligated to:
a. provide services as quickly as possible and free of charge to Victims;
b. organize Protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to Medical Rehabilitation, Social Rehabilitation, Repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;
c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and accompanying volunteers needed for Victims;
d. establish networks with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of Victims and cooperate with witness and Victim Protection institutions, social shelters or trauma centers owned by the government, community, or other institutions for the health recovery of Victims;
e. provide convenience, comfort, and safety for Victims;
f. maintain confidentiality of Victims;
g. provide economic empowerment;
h. provide fulfillment of legal assistance for Victims;
i. in the event that integrated service delivery is carried out through networking, the Integrated Service Center remains responsible for the entire referral process of services required by Victims.
(2) The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with Minimum Service Standards.

Article 11
(1) The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility to provide Protection to Women and Children Victims of Violence.
(2) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
 a. implementing protection policies for women and children victims of violence applied by the government;
 b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children victims of violence;
 c. collecting data and information about women and children victims of violence;
 d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;
 e. establishing an Integrated Service Center;
 f. allocating a budget for the implementation of Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;
 g. fostering, supervising, and evaluating Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence.

Article 16
Minimum Service Standards for the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence include the following services:
a. handling complaints/reports of Victims of Violence against Women and Children;
b. health services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
c. law enforcement and legal assistance for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
d. return for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;
f. psychological services for Women and Children Victims of Violence;

Article 10 point b is implemented in the form of community participation.
(2) The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. preventing the occurrence of acts of Violence against victims;
b. establishing complaint posts at the kelurahan/village level;
c. establishing women and children protection units within community organizations;
d. independently conducting socialization of women's rights and children's rights;
e. providing first aid to victims; and
f. reporting to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector, and mass media.
(4) The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 20
(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of violence, are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. return;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. psychological services; and
g. economic empowerment.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other regency/city governments, community, family and parents.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services, and handling of women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent's Regulation.

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF KONAWE KEPULAUAN,
a. that every citizen has the right to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and has the right to obtain security and freedom from all forms of violence;
b. that protection of women and children victims of violence is one aspect of the duties and responsibilities of regional government as mandated by law in providing protection and services to the community;
c. that the Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for women and children victims of violence, it is necessary to regulate the handling of women and children victims of violence in the region;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c above, it is deemed necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence;
In View of: 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Children (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3243);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6967);
7. Law Number 13 of 2013 concerning the Establishment of Konawe Kepulauan Regency in Southeast Sulawesi Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2013 Number 84, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5415);
8. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 5587), as has been amended several times by Law Number 11 of 2021 Concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of 2020 Number 245 Supplement to the State Gazette Number 6573);
9. Government Regulation Number 44 of 2017 concerning Implementation of Child Care (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2017 Number 220, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6132);
With Mutual Agreement

Article 23
The provision of services and assistance to victims shall be carried out with the principles of:
a. swift, safe, and empathetic;
b. guarantee of confidentiality;
c. easily accessible; and
d. free of charge.

CHAPTER III
FORMS OF VIOLENCE
Article 3
Forms of violence against women and children consist of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.
